Q: Sending a signed XML works in Windows but not on linux docker A: <p>Why XML Signatures Fail on Linux but Work on Windows</p>
<p>The <strong><code>alert-signature-not-valid</code></strong> error on Linux while Windows works is a <strong>classic cross-platform XML canonicalization issue</strong>. Here are the most common causes and fixes:</p>
<hr />
<h3>Root Cause 1: Line Ending Differences (Most Common)</h3>
<p>Windows uses <strong><code>\r\n</code></strong> (CRLF), Linux uses <strong><code>\n</code></strong> (LF). XML canonicalization is <strong>extremely sensitive</strong> to whitespace and line endings.</p>
<pre><code>csharp
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// Add this normalization BEFORE signing
private string NormalizeXmlLineEndings(string xml)
{
    // Normalize to LF only - critical for cross-platform XML signing
    return xml.Replace(&quot;\r\n&quot;, &quot;\n&quot;).Replace(&quot;\r&quot;, &quot;\n&quot;);
}

// In your PostSignedAlertXml method, normalize before signing:
var alertXml = SerializeAlertToXml(signedAlert);
alertXml = NormalizeXmlLineEndings(alertXml); // Add this line

var soapXml = NormalizeXmlLineEndings($@&quot;...&quot;);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>Root Cause 2: XML Serializer Produces Different Output on Linux</h3>
<p>The <strong><code>XmlSerializer</code></strong> on Linux (.NET on Linux) may produce different attribute ordering or namespace declarations than on Windows.</p>
<pre><code>csharp
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// Instead of XmlSerializer, use explicit XmlWriter settings for consistency
private string SerializeAlertToXml(alert signedAlert)
{
    var settings = new System.Xml.XmlWriterSettings
    {
        Encoding = new System.Text.UTF8Encoding(false), // No BOM!
        Indent = false,       // NO indentation - whitespace changes signature
        NewLineHandling = System.Xml.NewLineHandling.None, // Critical!
        NewLineChars = &quot;\n&quot;,  // Force LF
        OmitXmlDeclaration = true
    };

    using var ms = new System.IO.MemoryStream();
    using var writer = System.Xml.XmlWriter.Create(ms, settings);

    var serializer = new System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer(typeof(alert));
    serializer.Serialize(writer, signedAlert);
    writer.Flush();

    return System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetString(ms.ToArray());
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>Root Cause 3: Certificate Private Key Not Accessible on Linux</h3>
<p>On Linux, the certificate store works differently. The private key may load but <strong>fail silently</strong> during signing, producing an invalid signature.</p>
<pre><code>csharp
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// Add this diagnostic check before signing
private void ValidateCertificateForSigning(X509Certificate2 cert)
{
    _logger.Information(&quot;Cert Subject: {Subject}&quot;, cert.Subject);
    _logger.Information(&quot;Has Private Key: {HasKey}&quot;, cert.HasPrivateKey);

    if (!cert.HasPrivateKey)
        throw new InvalidOperationException(&quot;Certificate has no private key!&quot;);

    // Verify the private key is actually usable
    try
    {
        using var rsa = cert.GetRSAPrivateKey();
        if (rsa == null)
            throw new InvalidOperationException(&quot;RSA private key is null!&quot;);

        // Test sign a small piece of data
        var testData = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(&quot;test&quot;);
        var testSig = rsa.SignData(
            testData, 
            System.Security.Cryptography.HashAlgorithmName.SHA256,
            System.Security.Cryptography.RSASignaturePadding.Pkcs1
        );
        _logger.Information(&quot;Private key test signing succeeded, sig length: {Len}&quot;, testSig.Length);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        _logger.Error(&quot;Private key test FAILED: {Error}&quot;, ex.Message);
        throw;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>Root Cause 4: Certificate Must Be Loaded with <strong><code>Exportable</code></strong> Flag on Linux</h3>
<pre><code>csharp
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// When loading the certificate from PFX on Linux:

// ❌ Wrong - may not give access to private key on Linux
var cert = new X509Certificate2(&quot;cert.pfx&quot;, &quot;password&quot;);

// ✅ Correct - explicitly mark as exportable and machine key set
var cert = new X509Certificate2(
    &quot;cert.pfx&quot;,
    &quot;password&quot;,
    X509KeyStorageFlags.Exportable |
    X509KeyStorageFlags.MachineKeySet |    // Try this on Linux
    X509KeyStorageFlags.PersistKeySet
);

// Or for Docker environments:
var cert = new X509Certificate2(
    &quot;cert.pfx&quot;,
    &quot;password&quot;,
    X509KeyStorageFlags.Exportable |
    X509KeyStorageFlags.EphemeralKeySet   // Best for Docker/containers
);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>Root Cause 5: Namespace Handling Differences in SignedXml</h3>
<pre><code>csharp
</code></pre>
<pre><code>public class AlertSigner
{
    public static alert AddSignature(alert alertObj, X509Certificate2 cert)
    {
        // Serialize to XML first
        var xmlDoc = new System.Xml.XmlDocument();
        xmlDoc.PreserveWhitespace = false; // Set to false for consistent behavior

        var serialized = SerializeToString(alertObj);
        xmlDoc.LoadXml(serialized);

        var signedXml = new System.Security.Cryptography.Xml.SignedXml(xmlDoc);

        // Use RSA key directly - more reliable cross-platform than cert.PrivateKey
        using var rsa = cert.GetRSAPrivateKey();
        signedXml.SigningKey = rsa;

        // Explicitly set canonicalization
        signedXml.SignedInfo.CanonicalizationMethod = 
            System.Security.Cryptography.Xml.SignedXml.XmlDsigExcC14NTransformUrl;
        signedXml.SignedInfo.SignatureMethod = 
            &quot;http://www.w3.org/2001/04/xmldsig-more#rsa-sha256&quot;;

        var reference = new System.Security.Cryptography.Xml.Reference();
        reference.Uri = &quot;&quot;;
        reference.DigestMethod = &quot;http://www.w3.org/2001/04/xmlenc#sha256&quot;;

        reference.AddTransform(
            new System.Security.Cryptography.Xml.XmlDsigEnvelopedSignatureTransform()
        );
        reference.AddTransform(
            new System.Security.Cryptography.Xml.XmlDsigExcC14NTransform()
        );

        signedXml.AddReference(reference);
        signedXml.ComputeSignature();

        // ... rest of signing
    }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>Diagnostic Code to Find Your Exact Issue</h3>
<p>Add this before sending to compare outputs between Windows and Linux:</p>
<pre><code>csharp
</code></pre>
<pre><code>private void LogSignatureDebugInfo(XmlDocument signedDoc)
{
    _logger.Information(&quot;=== SIGNATURE DEBUG ===&quot;);

    // Log the exact bytes being sent (helps compare Win vs Linux)
    var bytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(signedDoc.OuterXml);
    _logger.Information(&quot;Document byte length: {Len}&quot;, bytes.Length);

    // Check for BOM
    bool hasBOM = bytes.Length &gt;= 3 &amp;&amp; bytes[0] == 0xEF &amp;&amp; bytes[1] == 0xBB &amp;&amp; bytes[2] == 0xBF;
    _logger.Information(&quot;Has UTF-8 BOM: {HasBOM}&quot;, hasBOM); // BOM will break signatures!

    // Check line endings
    bool hasCRLF = signedDoc.OuterXml.Contains(&quot;\r\n&quot;);
    bool hasCR = signedDoc.OuterXml.Contains(&quot;\r&quot;);
    _logger.Information(&quot;Has CRLF: {CRLF}, Has CR: {CR}&quot;, hasCRLF, hasCR);

    // Log digest values for comparison
    var nsMgr = new System.Xml.XmlNamespaceManager(signedDoc.NameTable);
    nsMgr.AddNamespace(&quot;ds&quot;, &quot;http://www.w3.org/2000/09/xmldsig#&quot;);

    var digestNodes = signedDoc.SelectNodes(&quot;//ds:DigestValue&quot;, nsMgr);
    foreach (XmlNode node in digestNodes)
    {
        _logger.Information(&quot;DigestValue: {Value}&quot;, node.InnerText);
    }

    var sigNodes = signedDoc.SelectNodes(&quot;//ds:SignatureValue&quot;, nsMgr);
    foreach (XmlNode node in sigNodes)
    {
        _logger.Information(&quot;SignatureValue (first 20 chars): {Value}&quot;, 
            node.InnerText.Substring(0, Math.Min(20, node.InnerText.Length)));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p><strong>Start with the <code>EphemeralKeySet</code> flag and line ending normalization</strong> — these resolve 90% of Windows-to-Linux XML signing issues in Docker containers.</p>

Q: TypeScript Error {imported module XXX} is not a function in Next.js App Router A: <p>The error says it. You are importing your <code>projects</code> variable which is indeed <strong>not</strong> a function but a variable.</p>
<pre><code>import { projects } from &quot;../../lib/data&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>In the code you are actually using it as a function, see this snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>export default async function BlogPost({ params }: Props) {

    // on this next line you are using the imported &quot;projects&quot; variable as a     //function, but it is just plain data aka a variable right
     const project = await projects(params.slug); 

    return (
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;{project.client}&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;{project.summary}&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Fix:</p>
<pre class="lang-typescript prettyprint-override"><code>export default async function BlogPost({ params }: Props) {
    // in the find function you hvae to adjust how to find you single project. 
    // I am assuming the &quot;slug&quot; should be the project id? &gt; 
    const project = projects.find(pr =&gt; pr.id === params.slug) 

    return (
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;{project.client}&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;{project.summary}&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    );
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to remove duplicates from a std::vector containing multiple types in C++? A: <p>Like already mentioned in the comments, the error is about <code>std::cout</code>-ing the elements of type <code>std::variant</code>. If you want to make your <code>valueRemoveTemplate</code> compatible with any streamable type and with <code>std::variant</code> holding streamable types, you can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/if.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">constexpr if</a>.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt; struct is_variant : std::false_type {};

template&lt;typename ...Args&gt;
struct is_variant&lt;std::variant&lt;Args...&gt;&gt; : std::true_type {};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
inline constexpr bool is_variant_v=is_variant&lt;T&gt;::value;

template &lt;typename T&gt; void valueRemoveTemplate(std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp; vec){
    std::sort(vec.begin(), vec.end());
    auto last = std::unique(vec.begin(),vec.end());
    vec.erase(last, vec.end());
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;No duplicates vector 3: &quot;;
    for (auto i : vec) {
        if constexpr (is_variant_v&lt;T&gt;) {
            std::visit([](auto&amp;&amp; x) { std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot;; }, i);
        } else {
            std::cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot;;
        }
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Credits for <code>is_variant</code> type trait to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/57134598/4165552">https://stackoverflow.com/a/57134598/4165552</a></p>
